# Acceptance record: round 1

**Conclusion: accepted.** The assembled delivery at game-developer `64cdad2a466e670275fbc0bf69b4c9afac1b99d8` + artist
`144b1149ff7aa4cd2bfbd8e88e3572183f399cdd` (assignment/brief at producer `8685f2d843f6c0c7c24152c334a77f9786f5b552` and later,
unchanged) is the game the input asks for: *复刻经典版本的超级马里奥 1，做成网页游戏*, recreating classic Super Mario Bros. 1 as a
web game. It meets the brief's P1 bar (World 1-1 complete and faithful) and P2 (World 1-2 to 1-4 playable to the end). P3 (later
worlds) is not part of this round, as the brief settles. This is the same content as release `build-5` in `world/producer`
(`build-5.zip`, sha256 f4ce1ab2fe7021ffa7a83481d6cd23b699325494bbc0a496c65ee309f2d1404c).

## What I inspected and played (producer, headless Chromium 1280 with Playwright, file://)
- The assembled delivery (`ht-role delivery`) and the unzipped build-5 zip both start from `index.html` with no server, no network,
  no page errors and no missing-asset warnings.
- Developer suites on the delivery: levels 27/27, sound_input 7/7, mechanics 20/20, mechanics2 26/26, mechanics3 19/19.
- The scripted bot plays 1-1 from the title to the flag with 0 deaths and goes into 1-2.
- I ran 1-1 by keyboard input from the title (run with B, jump with A).
- Screens checked by eye (`notes/shots/b5_grid.png`): title, 1-1, the 1-2 warp zone, the 1-3 start, Bowser on the 1-4 bridge, and
  Toad's "THANK YOU MARIO!". They read as SMB1: NES palette, 256×240, crisp integer scaling, the original HUD.
- Earlier builds: build-1 to build-4 (`notes/builds.md`).

